        title: "Funding arrears and the assessed-contributions cap as leverage",
        explanation:
          "Now, turn to a quieter, budgetary form of the same underlying leverage. The United States is the largest single funder of the UN system's assessed contributions, the mandatory dues each member pays according to a formula based on its share of global GDP, and has periodically used its funding role as direct leverage over the institution's policies, most formally through the 1999 Helms-Biden Act, which conditioned the payment of substantial US arrears on specific UN reforms, including a cap limiting the US assessed share of the regular budget to 22 percent even though the GDP-based formula would place it meaningfully higher. The United States has also accumulated significant arrears to the UN system at various points, sometimes for genuinely contested policy reasons and sometimes simply because Congress declined to appropriate the full assessed amount in a given budget cycle, a recurring pattern that leaves the UN system's day-to-day operations exposed to swings in US domestic budget politics that have nothing to do with the underlying policy dispute, if any, driving the shortfall.",
        whyItMatters:
          "This shows a dominant funder exercising leverage through the institution's own financial dependency rather than through its formal vote, since the General Assembly operates on a one-country-one-vote basis where the US holds no special voting weight at all, making the funding lever one of the only truly outsized tools available to it there. You should connect this directly back to the Bretton Woods weighted-voting concept: at the IMF and World Bank, American influence is embedded in the voting formula itself, while at the General Assembly and most specialized UN agencies, where voting is formally equal, funding arrears and the threat of withholding dues function as the practical substitute. For anyone assessing how much real pressure a funding threat can apply, the UN system's chronic cash-flow fragility, which forces real operational cutbacks whenever a major funder's payment is delayed, means this lever is genuinely effective even though it requires no formal institutional authority to use at all.",
        example:
          "The United States withdrew from UNESCO in 1984 partly over budgetary and governance disputes, rejoined in 2003, and withdrew again in 2017 and 2018 over separate concerns, before rejoining once more in 2023, a pattern of exit, funding withholding, and reentry that shows this leverage being exercised repeatedly against the same specific agency over four decades. US arrears to UN peacekeeping specifically have periodically run into the hundreds of millions of dollars, forcing troop-contributing countries, often far smaller economies than the United States, to effectively carry the shortfall for extended periods while Washington's own domestic budget politics worked themselves out. The Trump administration's 2025 decision to again withhold significant UN funding over specific agency disputes continued this same long-running pattern, confirming it as a durable feature of American institutional behavior across administrations of both parties rather than a one-off episode.",
