    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: is the United States bound by this institution, or using it as one tool among several to get what it would likely pursue anyway. The UN Charter's veto architecture and the Senate's own treaty power are the two founding constraints that shape everything that follows, one international and one domestic, both built in 1945-1949 specifically so that joining a binding multilateral order would not cost Washington control it was unwilling to surrender. NATO's Article 5 and Article 4 show the identical hedging instinct applied to collective defense, a political commitment dressed in legal language rather than an automatic trigger, while the WTO's disabled Appellate Body shows a founding member disabling an institution from within using entirely legitimate procedural tools rather than ever formally withdrawing. Weighted voting at the IMF and World Bank is the clearest case of formal, treaty-embedded American influence in this entire lecture, while sanctions leverage through the dollar and funding arrears inside the UN system show the identical dominance exercised informally, through infrastructure and budget dependency, at bodies where the formal voting rules grant the US no special weight at all. The exit-and-reentry pattern ties the formal and informal threads together, showing how little durability an American commitment carries once you know whether it ever cleared the Senate, and the G7/G20's rise alongside the OAS's own mixed record closes the lecture on two genuinely different answers to the same underlying frustration with treaty-bound friction, one building new, deliberately informal venues from scratch, the other inheriting a formally equal regional body that American funding and agenda-setting power have never fully let become so in practice. As you follow any live institutional story from here forward, ask first whether the relevant commitment was ever ratified, then whether the institution's own voting or veto structure gives Washington special formal weight, and only then judge how much the outcome actually reflects genuine multilateral constraint versus American leverage wearing an institutional label.",
