    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: does this institutional arrangement let France act with its own sovereign capability intact, or does it quietly fold French decision-making into a collective process Paris cannot fully control. The permanent Security Council seat and veto are the clearest expression of sovereign capability institutionally entrenched, a status no reform process has ever successfully touched, while France's weighted Council vote under EU qualified majority voting shows the same country operating under a genuinely shared, population-driven formula where its influence, though substantial, depends on coalition partners, Germany above all. The Conseil constitutionnel's referendum-or-Congress choice is the domestic hinge connecting these two logics, since French governments have learned, since 2005, to prefer the more controllable Congress route precisely because a referendum reintroduces the kind of unconstrained popular sovereignty the rest of this lecture's institutional machinery is designed to channel rather than confront directly. France's four-decade exit from and return to NATO's integrated command and its sustained push for European strategic autonomy are the same Gaullist sovereignty instinct applied first to an alliance and then projected outward into an entire EU institutional agenda, while the rotating EU presidency and the WTO-EU agricultural dual-track show France using genuinely available, time-limited, or indirect institutional levers to shape outcomes even where its underlying vote share alone would not be decisive. La Francophonie, finally, shows France building an entirely separate, nominally autonomous institutional architecture precisely because EU and UN structures alone cannot fully capture France's own distinct historical and linguistic reach. As you follow any real French institutional decision from here forward, ask first whether France is acting through a body where its sovereign capability is formally entrenched, as at the Security Council, or through one where its influence depends on coalition-building, as at the EU Council, since that distinction predicts far more about how Paris will actually behave than any single policy statement does.",
